  "title": "Chapter 18 – Types of Current: AC and DC",
  "order": 16,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "uem78tj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Direct Current (DC) flows continuously in a single direction with a constant magnitude and zero frequency.",
        "Alternating Current (AC) periodically reverses its direction and alters its magnitude continuously with time.",
        "In India, the frequency of domestic AC supply is 50 Hz, meaning current changes direction 100 times per second.",
        "AC is preferred for long-distance transmission because it can be stepped up or down efficiently using transformers.",
        "Batteries, solar cells, and thermocouples produce DC, whereas alternators in power plants produce AC."
      ],
      "id": "dydvo6b"
    },
